# LIVE REGRESSION FIXTURE (graduated out of `known_gaps/`) — the
# `&`-in-an-OWNING-POSITION class, costume 5 of 7: an ARRAY-LITERAL ELEMENT
# (`[&c.fd]`).
#
# REJECTED at `gg check` as intended. An array-literal element position is an
# ownership boundary — the array must own its elements — so there is nothing to
# write through to, and D31 full-strict gives `&` no other meaning.
#
# ⚠ THE CODE IT FIRES IS NOT THE CODE THIS CLASS IS OWED. The reject arrives as
# `error[E_AmpInOperandPosition]` — the OPERAND-position rule, from the
# chokepoint at `src/semantic/safety/check_expr.rs`. It is correct and
# actionable here (an array-literal element has no callee, which is exactly what
# that message says), but it is not a dedicated ownership-BOUNDARY diagnostic,
# and three siblings of this class — ctor / push / enum payload — are STILL
# ACCEPTED at HEAD because they DO have a callee. Do not read this fixture's
# green as the class being closed; the parent TODO row stays open.
#
# ⚠ THIS COSTUME WAS NOT "the sigil is ignored" — IT WAS A GARBAGE-VALUE
# MISCOMPILE. Before the reject, `gg check` was clean and both backends accepted,
# then stored a RAW STACK ADDRESS into an `int` slot:
#     C    -> 281474930661984
#     LLVM -> 281474478852760
# (the exact digits are ASLR-dependent; what was stable is that the value is a
# pointer, not 10). `ggdef` REJECTS it too ("`&`-alias into an owning position is
# not valid").
#
# The `fd` seed is COMPUTED, not a literal, so the address cannot be
# const-folded into a plausible-looking constant.
#
# The DROP facet of this same costume has its own fixture:
# `security/sound_amp_array_literal_duplicate_drop.gg` (a bare-param root plus a
# user `Drop` runs the drop TWICE on both backends).
#
# See `sound_amp_owning_position_ctor.gg` for the full sibling list and the
# ruling this class rests on.
struct Counter:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

void main():
    Counter c = Counter(seed())
    Vector[int] arr = [&c.fd]
    print(arr[0])
